{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789812341,"nonce":"ygLkMkJVAhdUMLhJOmVdr3K7MNc=","title":"Compiled list[negative] reads element 0 and list[1.5] reads element 1","message":"`wren_subscript_get` and `wren_subscript_set`, the helpers every compiled `list[i]` reaches that the inline paths do not cover, converted the index with `n as usize`: -1 saturates to 0 and 1.5 truncates to 1, so compiled code answered `xs[-1]` with `xs[0]` and `xs[1.5]` with `xs[1]` instead of the last element and the \"Subscript must be an integer\" error.\n\nReproducer: `var xs = [1, 2, 3]` then a loop of `xs[-1]` for 200k iterations at module level or in a method; once the loop is OSR-entered the value flips to 1. `--mode jit` (no OSR) and the interpreter answer 3 for every iteration, which hid it from the spec corpus. `fast_list_index` beside the helpers already did the right thing for the native list fast path; the helpers use it now, and a non-integral or out-of-range index falls through to the class's `[_]` method for the error.","files":null}]}